# mocari 0.4.1 uploads geometry one drawable at a time, and rejects the mask
# changes that a real rig produces every frame.
#
# Two separate problems in `render/wgpu/buffers.rs`, measured on
# DeepSeek/大肥鱼 (715 drawables, 58 967 vertices, 640x640 window):
#
# 1. Every drawable owns its own vertex buffer, so a frame that deforms the whole
#    model issues 715 `queue.write_buffer` calls - one staging copy and one copy
#    command each. Measured: 12-16 ms of CPU per frame for 2.6 MB of vertices.
#
# 2. `update_drawables` returns an error when a drawable's mask set, inverted-mask
#    flag, texture index or blend mode changes, because those were validated as
#    "the GPU state cannot absorb this". A rig with dynamic masks hits that
#    constantly - drawable 373 of this model switches its masks on every frame -
#    and the caller's only option is to rebuild *every* buffer, which is what
#    made the frame cost 18 ms of upload time.
#
#    None of those four values live in the vertex or index buffers. They only
#    feed the draw loop and the clipping plan, so they can be applied in place and
#    reported through `WgpuMeshUpdate::structure_changed()`; the caller then
#    rebuilds clipping resources and nothing else.
#
# The fix keeps one shared vertex buffer with a byte span per drawable. Drawables
# are packed back to back, so any run of them is contiguous and one `write_buffer`
# covers the whole dirty range. `WgpuDrawableBuffers::vertex_buffer()` now returns
# a `BufferSlice` instead of `&wgpu::Buffer`; `draw.rs` binds it directly.
#
# Measured on DeepSeek/大肥鱼 with the cursor sweeping the window:
#   before: upload 18.0 ms/frame, 1.6-1.9 animation steps per frame (~35 fps)
#   after:  upload  1.2 ms/frame, 1.00 animation steps per frame (60 fps)
# Rendering is unchanged: offscreen captures of both this rig and Rice are
# pixel-identical to the unpatched build.
#
# Apply with:
#   powershell -File scripts/vendor-mocari.ps1
#
# Worth reporting upstream: both changes are local to buffers.rs and draw.rs.
